#!/usr/bin/env -S uv run --script
# /// script
# requires-python = ">=3.11"
# dependencies = [
#     "httpx>=0.27",
#     "capabilities-contract==0.5.1",
# ]
# ///
"""
capabilities — the capability manager.

This tool is designed to be invoked by agents. Run `capabilities help` to load
the full usage contract on demand.

The manager is the engine of the capability system: it installs capability
scripts into the machine registry, snapshots their self-descriptions, gates
them through project/global policy, composes the session context injection, and verifies the
whole arrangement. It is the SOLE WRITER of the shared surfaces it manages —
the machine registry (~/.capabilities/<name>/), the project/global policy
collection, the project capabilities/.gitignore, the global skill
(~/.capabilities/.manager/skill/, symlinked into each host), and the
host hook wiring. Capability scripts never write any of these.

ENVIRONMENT
  CAPABILITIES_HOME    machine registry root        (default ~/.capabilities)
  CAPABILITIES_SOURCE  catalogue base URL           (default the public repo, raw)
  CAPABILITIES_BIN     PATH dir for symlinks        (default every directory already
                                                     holding the manager's own link,
                                                     on PATH or ~/.local/bin or ~/bin;
                                                     else ~/.local/bin or ~/bin,
                                                     whichever exists and is on PATH)
  CAPABILITIES_CONTEXT_PROMPT
                       optional project .env(.local) or process-env override
                       for the generated agent instruction paragraph.
  CAPABILITIES_PROJECT_ENVELOPE
                       the project envelope location, supplied by the project's
                       context owner in advance of the lookup below.
  CAPABILITIES_PROJECT_ENVELOPE_ROOT
                       the project root the value above answers for. A capability
                       sets both when it hands its resolved answer down to the
                       processes it spawns, so a descendant standing in another
                       project passes that handoff over and resolves its own.
                       Alone it carries no answer; an envelope supplied without
                       it is honored wherever it is inherited.
  CAPABILITIES_PROJECT_ID
                       the project id a write may stamp, handed down beside the
                       envelope by a process that read it from project.json, so
                       a descendant uses it as given; one that differs from the
                       project.json it finds refuses writes.
  CAPABILITIES_PROJECT_ID_ROOT
                       the project root that id answers for, scoping it the way
                       CAPABILITIES_PROJECT_ENVELOPE_ROOT scopes the envelope.
  CAPABILITIES_READ_ONLY
                       `1` or `true` (any case) puts the process and everything
                       it starts under the read-only switch: every capability
                       reads but changes nothing (write verbs, project-record
                       writes and service activation exit 4), and this manager
                       refuses agent set, allow, connections import, disable,
                       enable, inherit, init, node add, node remove, node set,
                       quarantine, relabel, set, store set and store unset.
                       doctor reports it. Unset, `0` or `false` leaves it off.
  AGENTKIT_DB_URL, AGENTKIT_DB_HOST, AGENTKIT_DB_PORT, AGENTKIT_DB_NAME,
  AGENTKIT_DB_USER, AGENTKIT_DB_PASSWORD, AGENTKIT_DB_SCHEMA,
  AGENTKIT_DB_SSLMODE, AGENTKIT_DB_SSLROOTCERT, AGENTKIT_DB_TRANSACTION_HOST,
  AGENTKIT_DB_TRANSACTION_PORT
                       the environment level of the database cascade, below a
                       project's .env.local/.env and above the machine's store
                       setting (see STORE VERBS).

PROJECT PATH VERB
  path --json [--identity]
        Resolve the current project root and capability envelope once, returning
        project_root, project_envelope, and provider. Plain projects use
        <root>/capabilities. ContextKit-bound projects must return a usable
        absolute in-project path; a broken binding is an error, never a fallback.
        --identity adds project_identity: the project id, read from the
        envelope's project.json alone, and its state - declared (project.json
        holds it), undeclared (no id), handed (handed down by the launching
        process as CAPABILITIES_PROJECT_ID) or mismatch (handed down differently
        from project.json, which writes refuse; handed_id names it) - with
        project_json_id, bound (a .contextkit/config.toml is present) and
        contextkit_id (the id where bound, else null), the two fields
        capabilities stamped with an earlier contract read. Where project.json
        declares no id and
        .contextkit/config.toml holds one under [identity], that id is copied
        into project.json once (not under the read-only switch) and answered.

THE PROJECT ENVELOPE
  Every project file the system owns — identity, the gate, the state guard,
  and each capability's identifiers, references, connections, settings and
  state — sits in one envelope; records are kept in files, except the
  connections, grants, identifiers and policy of a project in store mode,
  which its store holds (see `connections import`). In a project
  bound by `.contextkit/config.toml` its location is read from that binding:
  `[body] root`, the folder holding the agent's body, and `[sources]
  capabilities` inside it (`.capabilities` names the legacy project-root
  envelope). Without that binding the envelope is <root>/capabilities. A
  binding that names an absolute, hidden or out-of-project path is an error
  (body_path_invalid); it never silently selects another tree. `path --json`
  reports the body root and each layer.

MACHINE VERBS
  install <name> [--from <path|url> | --source ID] [--yes] [--allow]
        Fetch the capability into the registry, symlink its executable onto
        PATH, snapshot `stub` + `manifest --json` (keyed by payload hash),
        scaffold credentials per the declared scope, surface declared
        post-install steps (--yes runs them), migrate its tables (see
        update), regenerate the skill.
        A capability new to this machine arrives quarantined (see allow);
        --allow installs it allowed. A core capability (see list) new to this
        machine and installed from the official catalogue - no --from, no
        --source - arrives allowed and enabled at global scope instead: as the
        store's global policy row (every agent, every node) where a database
        resolves, unless the store already holds a global row for it, which is
        kept, or the process runs under CAPABILITIES_READ_ONLY, which opens no
        gate. Install writes no policy file anywhere: where no database
        resolves or answers, nothing is written and the enable is reported
        pending, for `capabilities enable <name> --global` on the master. JSON
        then carries global_policy {enabled, gate} naming the store's table,
        or {enabled, pending, reason, hint}. Reinstalling or updating an
        installed capability keeps its machine state and writes no policy;
        uninstall drops the machine state.
        --from accepts: a script file, a bundle directory holding <name> or
        bin/<name>, a repo directory holding capabilities/<name>/bin/<name>, or
        a URL. Source directories, and script paths inside source directories,
        install the complete bundle, excluding bytecode/cache files. Default
        catalogue sources install from the public repo bundle when available.
        --source installs from one explicitly named registered source and records
        its repository/ref/commit provenance. An unqualified name remains an
        explicit request for the built-in official catalogue; custom sources
        never shadow it.
  uninstall <name> [--purge-credentials]
        Remove the PATH symlink and the registry entry; regenerate the skill.
        Credentials are kept and reported; --purge-credentials deletes the
        user-scope credential file after showing it.
  update [<name>]
        Re-fetch one capability (or all) from its recorded source; refresh
        snapshots; regenerate the skill.
        Install, update and the release that reconciles this machine run
        `<name> migrate` once the payload is in place, when its manifest
        declares tables in the machine's store, and report it as `migrate`
        {ok, applied, version} or {ok: false, exit, error}. A refusal - no
        store configured, store unreachable or busy, the capability
        quarantined - leaves the payload installed; its first call migrates.
  update --check [<name>] [--json]
        Report, for every installed capability or the one named, whether its
        recorded source carries a different payload. Writes no registry entry,
        snapshot or skill; the only thing it may bring forward is a registered
        source's cache, the tree `search` reads. Payloads are compared by their
        catalogue hash - the generated catalogue's payload_sha256, recomputed for
        the installed copy - and versions are the newest heading of each change
        log, null where none can be read. JSON {checked: [{name, source,
        installed {catalog_sha256, version, source_commit?},
        available {catalog_sha256, version} | null, update_available,
        error? {code, message, hint?}}], update_available}; the top-level
        update_available is true when any row's is. A source that cannot be
        reached or read leaves that row's available null and update_available
        false and names it in error; the other rows are still answered. Exit 0
        when every row was answered, behind or current alike; 5 when any row
        carries an error; 3 when the named capability is not installed. Output
        is always JSON, so --json changes nothing.
  self-update [--from <checkout>]
        Refresh the manager itself and re-point its PATH symlink (install.sh is
        the first-install bootstrap). By default fetch the canonical source;
        --from installs the manager and release assets from a local checkout.
        The incoming release manifest owns the asset list and hashes; the whole
        release is verified and staged before one atomic symlink switch. No-op
        when already current. The manager is not a capability, so it has no
        registry entry or snapshots.
  self-update --check [--from <checkout>] [--json]
        Report whether the canonical source (or --from) carries a manager
        release other than the one the PATH symlink selects. Reads only the
        incoming release manifest; stages, writes and switches nothing. A
        release is identified by its release id, the sha256 of its release
        manifest, which is the name self-update gives its release directory;
        the version is the newest heading of that release's manager change log,
        null where none can be read. JSON {installed {release, manager_sha256,
        version} | null, available {release, manager_sha256, version, source},
        update_available}; installed is null when the PATH symlink selects no
        versioned release, which self-update replaces. Exit 0 when the check
        answered, behind or current alike; 5 when the source cannot be reached
        or carries no valid release manifest. Output is always JSON, so --json
        changes nothing.
  allow <name> / quarantine <name>
        Set this machine's ceiling for an installed capability, kept in
        $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/capabilities/machine.json, which only the manager
        writes. Quarantined is effective-disabled in every project and outside
        any, whatever project or global policy says; allowed leaves the two
        scopes deciding. A capability with no entry is allowed. JSON
        {capability, machine: "allowed"|"quarantined", changed}. Exit 3 when
        not installed. Lifting a quarantine is the user's decision: an agent
        asks and never runs `allow` on its own initiative.
  skill
        Place the carried global skill and relink every agent host present on
        this machine. The bootstrap and every install/uninstall/update already
        do this; run it after installing a new agent host, or to repair the
        drift `doctor` reports.
  list
        Installed capabilities with summaries, tier, and explicit project/global
        gate, effective state, the scope that supplied it, and — in a project —
        the resolved envelope path. `tier` is on every row, "core" or
        "standard": the manager declares the catalogue's core set once, as
        CORE_CAPABILITIES in its own executable, and every other capability is
        standard. The tier is a mark to group by, and it acts only at first
        arrival (see install): there a core capability arrives allowed and
        enabled at global scope. After that it is gated, enabled and granted
        exactly as a standard one; a bundled service still needs an explicit
        project enable, and a connection still needs its grant. Source
        verification refuses a core name the catalogue does not carry. Every
        row carries `machine` ("allowed" or
        "quarantined") and `machine_connections`, the connections this
        machine's global scope declares for it less those whose grant there
        is disabled - once the config home holds no connections file and the
        store's global rows exist, the connections the store holds for it
        less those whose global grant is disabled; a quarantined row is
        effective "disabled" with source "machine".
  search [QUERY] [--source ID]
        Search cached catalogues from the official and registered sources.
        Listing never executes catalogue capability code. JSON {matches [{name,
        source, summary, installed}], unavailable_sources [{source, hint}]}.
        Every row carries installed: true when a capability of that name is in
        the machine registry, false when it is a catalogue capability not yet
        installed here.
  changelog (<name> | --manager | --contract) [--all]
            [--from <path> | --source ID]
        Print the change log of a capability, of the manager, or of the
        contract: what changed for someone who already has it. By default only
        the entries newer than the installed version; --all prints the whole
        log. The installed version is the newest entry in the installed copy's
        own log - the registry copy for a capability, the manager release on
        PATH for the manager and the contract. An installed copy without a log
        predates change logs, so every entry is newer than it; a capability not
        installed here prints its whole log. The log is read from where the
        update would come from: a capability's recorded source, else the
        official catalogue, and the manager's own source for the manager and
        the contract. --source reads one registered source's local tree, as
        `search` does (`source refresh` brings it forward); --from reads a
        local checkout or bundle. Read-only: it installs and writes nothing.
        JSON {target, kind, log, installed {version, log,
        registry|release, source_commit?} | null, view, entries [{heading,
        body}], note?}. Exit 3 when the source carries no log, 5 when it
        cannot be reached.
  source init ID [--remote GIT_URL]
        Create the canonical editable source workspace at
        ~/.capabilities/sources/ID, initialize git, vendor the authoring contract,
        register it, and emit the exact authoring/publishing path.
  source add ID GIT_URL [--ref REF]
        Register and fetch a remote capability source into the manager-owned
        read-only cache. Existing git/SSH credentials are used; no token is stored.
  source clone ID GIT_URL [--ref REF]
        Clone an existing source into the canonical editable workspace and
        register it for authoring on this machine.
  source register-checkout official
        Register this exact primary official checkout for consumer-originated
        development. Must be invoked as ./bin/capabilities from that checkout.
  source list | path ID | refresh [ID] | sync ID | check ID | remove ID
        Inspect sources, resolve the one editable path, refresh remote caches,
        compare declarations, payload hashes and the generated catalogue, or
        unregister a source. Authoring audit stays explicit.
        `check` writes nothing, so it reads any registered source — an editable
        workspace or a fetched cache — from any directory. Exit 0 clean, 7 drift.
        Removing never deletes an editable workspace.
  source index ID [--staged]
        Deterministically rebuild the generated catalogue. --staged derives it
        from the Git index and stages the result, so ignored, unstaged, and
        temporary files cannot influence the candidate commit. It never audits.
  source verify ID --ref REF [--base REF]
        Verify one immutable Git tree. With --base, derive the direct manager and
        capability checks from the diff; without it, use the full safe fallback.
        Each selected capability is audited once. This is the publication/CI invariant.
  source release ID --ref REF
        Publish one already-committed, verified descendant of the clean source
        checkout's tracked branch; fast-forward the checkout, reconcile locally
        installed changed payloads, and update the manager when it changed.

STORE VERBS
  Which PostgreSQL a project's capabilities keep their tables in is resolved
  per project through one cascade, by the shared database library
  capabilities-contract (capabilities_contract.db) and nowhere else; the
  first level that answers is used whole:
    project      the AGENTKIT_DB_* keys in the project's .env.local and .env,
                 .env.local winning key by key;
    environment  the same keys in the process environment;
    machine      this machine's store setting file,
                 $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/agentkit/store.json
                 (~/.config/agentkit/store.json when XDG_CONFIG_HOME is unset),
                 an agentkit.store.v1 document holding host, port, database,
                 user, password, sslmode, an optional sslrootcert, an
                 optional db_schema (default agentkit) and an optional
                 transaction_host and transaction_port, at mode 0600. Its
                 format is SHEBANG.md's "The store setting".
  Within the project or environment level AGENTKIT_DB_URL, a postgresql://
  URL, wins over that level's separate keys; AGENTKIT_DB_SCHEMA and the
  transaction address apply beside it.
  A store has one address unless the level in force also names a transaction
  address (AGENTKIT_DB_TRANSACTION_HOST and AGENTKIT_DB_TRANSACTION_PORT, or
  transaction_host and transaction_port in the file): the same store at
  another endpoint, typically a transaction-mode pooler, with the level's own
  credentials, database and schema; one naming only a port keeps the host.
  Then only the processes that hold LISTEN - a service's listener, a watch -
  connect to the address; every other connection - each command, the
  records reader, a lease renewal, a service's questions - goes to the
  transaction address and carries no session state. A hosted store with a
  session-mode and a transaction-mode pooler on one host takes the session
  pooler as its address and the transaction pooler's port as
  --transaction-port; a known transaction pooler as the address itself is
  refused (exit 6 transaction_pooler). An agent's records - its gate,
  connections, grants, identifiers and settings - are files only until it
  has moved into the store (see migrate); from then they are rows of the
  contract's tables in the store, beside the tables each capability migrates
  for itself. These verbs never create a table.
  store set --host H --database D --user U [--port P]
            [--sslmode require|verify-ca|verify-full|disable]
            [--sslrootcert PATH|system] [--schema NAME]
            [--transaction-host H] [--transaction-port P]
            (--password-stdin | --password-file PATH | --password-env KEY)
        Write the machine level: the setting file whole (a temporary file
        renamed over it, mode 0600) under the manager's lock, in the format
        the library reads, which is proven before the file is replaced. A
        file declaring a format version this manager does not know is left
        as it is (exit 6 store_setting_too_new). The password comes from
        stdin, a file or the named environment variable, never from argv; a
        --password flag is refused. --port defaults to 5432; --sslmode
        defaults to verify-full with --sslrootcert and to require without it;
        disable is admitted only for a local host (a Unix socket directory,
        localhost or a loopback address), and disable elsewhere, allow and
        prefer are refused (exit 6 sslmode_too_weak). --schema names the
        schema the setting binds: a lowercase identifier, never public,
        information_schema or pg_* (exit 6 bad_schema_name); without it the
        file names none and binds agentkit. --transaction-host and
        --transaction-port name the store's transaction address (either one
        alone keeps the other from the address); without them the store has
        one address. Exit 4 under the read-only
        switch. Answers as `store show` does for the current project, with
        changed.
  store show [--project PATH] [--json]
        Every level of the cascade for the current project, or for the
        project at PATH: what each level holds, its secrets redacted (a
        password as ***, a URL as the parameters libpq reads from it, never
        its text), the files or variables its values came from, and which
        level is in force. Reads only. JSON {configured, project, in_force,
        setting, error, levels {project {asked, answers, setting, error},
        environment {answers, setting, error}, machine {path, exists,
        answers, setting, error}}}: setting is the library's report of the
        level in force {level, sources, schema, transaction {host, port}?,
        url{...} | host, port, database, user, sslmode, sslrootcert,
        password}; error {code,
        message, hint} names a level the library refuses. Output is always
        JSON, so --json changes nothing.
  store doctor [--project PATH] [--json]
        Prove the database in force for the current project, or for the
        project at PATH, whichever level answered: a TLS connection works,
        reporting the server version and the negotiated TLS; a plain-text
        connection to the same server is refused, so a pg_hba.conf that lets
        plain text back in is noticed; and the role may use the setting's
        schema - USAGE and CREATE on it when it exists, CREATE on the
        database when it does not yet, since the first migration creates it.
        A database on a local host is not reached across a network: its
        plain-text check is skipped (skipped true), and with sslmode disable
        a working connection without TLS passes the tls check. A store
        that names a transaction address is also proven there: a transaction
        connection opened at it, over TLS unless sslmode is disable, runs a
        transaction bound to the setting's schema; a store with one address
        skips that check. Never prints a secret. JSON {ok, project,
        in_force, setting, levels, sslmode, db_schema, checks {tls {ok,
        server_version?, tls {version, cipher}?, reason?, error?},
        plain_text_refused {ok, reason, skipped?}, schema {ok, name, exists,
        reason}, transaction {ok, skipped?, host?, port?, tls?, reason?,
        error?}}}, setting and levels as `store show`.
        Exit 0 when all four hold, 7 when any fails, 3 no_store_setting when
        no level answers, 6 with the library's code when the level in force
        is refused.
  store unset
        Remove the machine level's setting file under the manager's lock. A
        file of a format version this manager does not know is left as it is
        (exit 6 store_setting_too_new). Exit 4 under the read-only switch.
        Answers as `store show` does, with changed; removing no setting is
        not an error.

DEVELOPMENT VERBS
  dev start <name> [--project PATH | --no-project] [--source ID-OR-PATH]
            [--session ID] [--allow-parallel]
        Create one isolated source worktree and attach the existing consumer
        checkout when run from it. The project is recorded but never owned,
        branched, cleaned or removed by the manager.
  dev install <session> <name> [<name> ...]
        Structurally validate and install capabilities from the session source
        worktree into its isolated registry. Re-run after source edits to refresh payloads.
  dev exec <session> [--cwd source] [--inherit-env KEY ...] -- <command>
        Execute one foreground command inside the session environment. Output
        and exit status are passed through. Parent env is denied by default;
        named keys may be inherited without putting values on argv. Where this
        machine resolves a database outside any project (its environment, then
        its store setting file), the session reaches that database bound to a
        schema of its own, dev_<session>, through the AGENTKIT_DB_* keys with
        AGENTKIT_DB_SCHEMA (which then cannot be inherited), and AGENT_NODE_NAME
        goes with it; without one the lane has no database, as before. The
        session creates that schema, marked as its own, before anything in
        the lane reaches it, and drops it when it ends - by finish, stop or
        gc - unless the schema was already there or no longer carries its
        mark; `database_schema` in the answer says which.
  dev run <session> <capability> [--project-only] -- [<arg> ...]
        Run exactly one session-installed capability against the attached live
        project through a mode-0700, per-invocation selected-input projection.
        For a project that reads its records from the store, where a database
        resolves, the projection is that capability's rows - the agent's
        registry row and nodes, its own and the global policy, connections,
        grants, identifiers and settings - copied into the session's schema,
        which the payload reads instead of the store's; otherwise a temporary
        config home with the selected gate and connection files.
  dev check <session>
        Derive validation scope from the recorded base across committed,
        staged, unstaged and untracked changes, then run each selected direct
        validator once without editing source or index.
  dev live start|status|stop <session> telegram
  dev live recover <session> telegram --restore-canonical
        Borrow one account's real Telegram state under a durable exclusive
        lease; recovery is explicit and always targets the recorded canonical state.
  dev doctor <session> | dev list
        Inspect provenance, worktrees, isolation paths, installed payloads, and
        cleanup readiness. Source dirtiness is visible development state, not
        by itself a failure.
  dev stop <session> [--discard-work]
        Remove only a clean session whose commits are absent, merged, or
        patch-equivalent to commits on its recorded base ref. Dirty or
        unpublished worktrees are preserved and refused. --discard-work
        removes an abandoned session by destroying exactly that work, its
        uncommitted changes and its unpublished commits, and names what it
        destroyed in the result. It never reaches a live takeover lease, a
        worktree git cannot report on, or an unmanaged path.
  dev finish <session>
        Require a clean prepared commit, release it through the existing
        source transaction, then remove only manager-owned session paths. The
        attached project is never cleaned, committed, published or removed.
  dev gc [--older-than 7d]
        Apply the same safe cleanup rule to old sessions; unsafe sessions are
        reported and retained.

POLICY VERBS
  enable <name> --project|--global [--node <node>]
  disable <name> --project|--global [--node <node>] [--deny]
        Write an explicit policy record at project or global scope through the
        active records backend. Files mode maps those scopes to
        capabilities/settings.json and $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/capabilities/settings.json;
        store mode to this project's row, stamped with its verified id, and the
        global row of capabilities_policy, which applies to every project
        reading that store, each for every node, or with --node for that one
        node (the name a node gives itself: AGENT_NODE_NAME, else its host
        name). A row naming this node is read before the row for every node,
        at either scope. Only the project's row naming a node places a
        capability's bundled service on that node; a row for every node, or a
        global row, makes the CLI usable there without placing the service
        (`deployment guide profiles`). Files carry no node, so --node in files
        mode is refused with node_requires_store; `capabilities connections
        import` moves the project's records into its store first. --deny takes
        no --node.
        Scope is mandatory: ask whether the decision is for this project or
        every project before invoking the manager. Project enable removes any
        harness deny rule; project disable --deny additionally writes
        permissions.deny Bash(<name>:*) into .claude/settings.json. Enabling a
        capability quarantined on this machine writes the entry and warns: it
        stays effective-disabled until the user allows it.
  inherit <name> --project|--global [--node <node>]
        Remove the selected scope's explicit entry. A project then inherits the
        machine default; removing the global entry restores default deny. With
        --node (store mode only) remove that scope's row for that one node,
        which stops a service placed there by it.

PROJECT VERBS (run inside a consuming project)
  init [--claude] [--codex]
        Wire the project: scaffold the durable project identity, the
        manager-owned capabilities/.gitignore, and settings.json only in files
        mode. A new project - no identity, no settings file, no legacy
        envelope - is a new agent and starts in the store: its identity
        declares records "store", its row is registered migrated with this
        node as master, and init refuses it, writing nothing, without
        AGENT_NODE_NAME (node_unnamed), without a store
        (store_not_configured), or on a store whose global records have not
        moved (global_not_migrated). An existing project is initialised as
        before. The identity's id is written once and never rewritten: an id
        `.contextkit/config.toml` holds under [identity] is carried over,
        otherwise one is minted offline. When a database resolves for the
        project, its agent row is registered in capabilities_agents (the
        result's `agent`: registered, store_not_configured, or the store's
        error, which never fails init); per host, wire the
        SessionStart hook and the generated context file and generate the
        skill. No flags defaults to Claude Code wiring; Codex projects must
        pass --codex. Host wiring is manager-owned: each init brings the hook
        entries and Codex config keys it owns to their current form, and
        refuses a host settings file it cannot parse rather than replace it.
        Idempotent. When `.contextkit/config.toml` is present, init keeps the
        envelope and skill setup but skips host wiring: ContextKit owns it.
        A legacy project `.capabilities/` envelope is preflighted and migrated
        into visible `capabilities/`; content conflicts refuse before writes.
  relabel <slug>
        Give this project a new label, keeping its id: the label lives in the
        project identity, and that file is the one write. Records are not
        moved and are not re-scoped — they never referred to the label; no
        database is touched. Renaming the directory is unrelated and needs no
        command. Idempotent.
  agent show [--icon-out PATH]
        This project's row in the agent registry (capabilities_agents in the
        project's store, keyed by the project.json id): {ok, project,
        agent_id, registered, agent, store {level, schema}}, where agent is
        null until the agent is registered, else {id, name, description,
        icon_sha, icon_bytes, onboarding_status, onboarding_routine,
        migrating_since, migrated_at, created_at, updated_at}. --icon-out
        writes the icon's PNG bytes to PATH (exit 6 no_icon without one).
  agent set [--name TEXT] [--description TEXT] [--icon PNG | --no-icon]
            [--onboarding-status pending|complete|none]
            [--onboarding-routine NAME]
        Register the agent where it is not yet registered and write the
        fields given; an empty TEXT or NAME, `none` and --no-icon clear a
        field. --icon takes a PNG file and stores it with its SHA-256.
        Answers {ok, project, agent_id, created, changed, agent, store};
        writing the values a row already holds changes nothing.
  node list
        The nodes of this project's agent (capabilities_agent_nodes): {ok,
        project, agent_id, nodes [{node, kind, address, is_master,
        may_write, target, last_seen_at}], store}.
  node add <node> --kind mac|server [--address HOST] [--target NAME]
           [--master] [--may-write]
        Register a node of this project's agent, registering the agent first
        where needed. <node> is the name the node gives itself in
        AGENT_NODE_NAME; --address is its fixed host name or IP, --target the
        deployment target that deploys it. An agent has at most one master:
        --master while another node is master exits 6 master_exists naming
        it. A node already registered exits 6 node_exists. Answers {ok,
        project, agent_id, node, changed, store}.
  node set <node> [--kind mac|server] [--address HOST] [--target NAME]
           [--master | --no-master] [--may-write | --no-may-write]
        Change a registered node's fields; an empty HOST or NAME clears it.
        Exit 6 node_unknown for a node not registered, master_exists as for
        add. Answers as add, changed false when nothing moved.
  node remove <node>
        Remove a registered node; answers {ok, project, agent_id, removed,
        store}. Exit 6 node_unknown for a node not registered.
        The agent and node verbs need the project's id (exit 6
        no_project_identity: run init) and a database that resolves for the
        project (exit 6 store_not_configured, 5 when it cannot be reached);
        set, add and remove exit 4 under the read-only switch.
  context --claude|--codex|--fragment
        Compose the session injection from the gate + snapshots + project
        envelopes. --fragment prints the manager-owned Markdown block without
        writing host files; context owners such as ContextKit insert this output
        verbatim. The host modes place the same block where the host reads it
        (--claude: .claude/rules/CAPABILITIES.md; --codex:
        .codex/generated/capabilities.md, which Codex loads through the root
        .capabilities.md link named by project_doc_fallback_filenames in
        .codex/config.toml) and replace the file whole. In a Codex-wired
        project --codex also creates or repairs that link and config entry,
        replacing the manager's earlier fallback names and keeping others.
        Composition reads the active records backend and degradation is
        per-capability, never whole. A capability whose manifest declares a
        `contribution` has that file, read from its own state folder in the
        project envelope and held to its declared byte ceiling, composed after
        its stub line; a contribution that is missing, unreadable, malformed
        or over its ceiling is replaced by one line naming `<name> render`,
        and costs nothing else. Directly above a composed contribution of
        `memory`, and nowhere else, the block carries the line
        `<!-- contextkit:memory-contribution -->`, on which ContextKit leaves
        out its own projection of the project's memory folder. Run by hand, a
        host mode prints the file it wrote and the enabled set. Run as the host's SessionStart hook (the
        harness pipes a payload whose hook_event_name is SessionStart), it
        prints nothing unless the session must be told: the file was just
        created or changed since the session loaded it, it could not be
        rebuilt, or Codex cannot load it because a root AGENTS.md or
        AGENTS.override.md takes precedence. The hook always exits 0, and the
        context reaches the agent through the host's own file loading, never
        through hook output. The generated agent instruction paragraph
        defaults to identifier persistence guidance and can be overridden with
        CAPABILITIES_CONTEXT_PROMPT in the project's .env/.env.local.
        Do not create or edit generated context files by hand; rerun this
        command after manual envelope changes. In a ContextKit-managed project
        (`.contextkit/config.toml`), ContextKit owns both host bindings and
        the combined context; the host modes delegate without writing and
        point to `contextkit build`, while --fragment remains the read-only
        provider surface ContextKit calls.
  build [--print] [--json]
        Compose a ContextKit-bound agent's generated host context as
        `contextkit build` does: its runtime and collaboration texts, guide
        menu, onboarding, foreign instruction files, inline and stub context
        (global context included), routine index and this manager's own
        capabilities block, composed in this process. --print writes the text
        to stdout and writes nothing; with --json it answers {project, output,
        text, warnings}. Without --print it replaces the configured output
        (`[output] context`, default .contextkit/generated/context.md) whole
        and points each host ContextKit has a hook for at it (.contextkit.md,
        .claude/rules/CONTEXT.md), leaving a path that holds something else
        blocked; it answers {project, output, created, changed, links,
        foreign_instructions, warnings}. An output that is not generated
        context exits 6 output_not_generated. No update notice is composed.
        A project without a ContextKit binding exits 6 not_contextkit_bound.
  inventory [NAME ...] [--detail] [--network]
        What this project's capabilities are and what they hold. One row per
        effective-enabled capability: its tier (as in `list`), gate and the
        scope that set it,
        the connections it may act on and the ones a grant withholds,
        the declared service and its state, and whether the
        capability reports an inventory of its own and how much of it needs
        the network. The survey runs only the capabilities that ship a
        service, each once for its service's state from its own local report
        (`unknown`, with the reason, when it cannot answer). A service row
        carries `state` (`running` while a process serves the project, its
        own or the machine service it joined), `detail`, and the verdict of
        the service's local checks: `ok` true when they find nothing wrong,
        false with a one-line `problem` when they do, null when the capability
        gives no verdict. The checks are the local part of its `service
        doctor`, with no network call and no model turn. --detail also asks
        each one for its own report — `<name> inventory`, local and effect-free
        — running them together, so a reader assembling a whole project spends
        one process per capability here instead of several in every caller.
        --network implies --detail and passes the caller's consent down, so a
        capability may then reach its service for detail a local read cannot
        supply. NAME limits the answer to named capabilities.
        `list` answers for the machine; this answers for the project.
  ids <name>
        Render <name>'s project identifiers as markdown — the full labelled
        list the session context points to. (`<name> ids get|set|rm` is the
        per-value data interface on the capability itself.)
  get <name> <collection> <key>
        Read one resolved configuration record from wherever this project keeps
        its records. Collections are connection, grant, identifier, setting,
        policy, and document. In store mode a grant is the row that applies on
        this node - the one naming it, else the one for every node - and adds
        node (null for every node) and is_default to its fields.
  set <name> <collection> <key> <json-or-string> [--note TEXT]
        Write one project-scoped human-owned configuration record. The writable
        collections here are connection, grant, and setting; policy stays with
        the policy verbs, identifiers with the capability's ids verbs, and long
        text with the capability's context verbs. In store mode a connection
        is a row every project may be granted, a grant is this project's row
        and may carry node (a node name, or null for every node, the default)
        and is_default (the default pointer, one per capability and node), and
        setting connection.default moves is_default; such a write stamps the
        project id, so it refuses (exit 6) where the id is not verified, and
        `records.mode` answers `store` with the table as `source`. A store
        that cannot be reached is exit 5 store_unreachable.
  connections import
        Move this project's records - connections, grants, identifiers and
        policy - from its files into its store (the database `store show`
        names for it) and the project into store mode, recorded as
        "records": "store" in its project.json, which only this verb writes;
        a project at "connections": "store" keeps only its connections and
        grants in the store, and its identifiers and policy in files, until
        this verb runs again. Every identity the project uses becomes a row
        of capabilities_connections: each one its envelope declares, and an
        identity of the config home only where the capability is enabled for
        the project and the project may use that identity or points its
        default at it. One the config home merely offers stays out of the
        store and is named under skipped; once the project uses it, the
        import run again from its files writes it, and a project already in
        store mode writes it with `set <name> connection <id> <spec>` and
        `set <name> grant <id> '{"enabled": true}'`. Every row written that
        the project may use, switched off itself, or points its default at
        gets a grant for every node in capabilities_connection_grants,
        stamped with the project id (verified, as any write that stamps it),
        carrying the decision its files resolve to (a field no scope decided
        stays null). Rows already in the store are never removed.
        The rows are written, and proven to resolve as the files do, in one
        transaction before project.json changes. A row the store already
        holds unchanged is left as it is, so running it again changes
        nothing; one that differs, or a grant of this project the files do
        not have, refuses the whole import (exit 6 import_conflict) and
        writes nothing. Each scope's identifiers and policy entries become
        rows of capabilities_identifiers and capabilities_policy, the
        envelope's under the project id and the config home's as global rows
        (agent_id null, for every project reading the store), and the store
        must resolve this project's identifiers and gate as its files do, each
        from the same scope: a held row that differs, a project row the files
        lack, or a global row that would change what this project resolves
        refuses the import as well. The files are kept and no longer read for
        those records. JSON {ok, project, agent_id, node, mode,
        previous_mode (files, connections or store), identity, store {level,
        sources, schema, tables}, capabilities {<name> {connections
        {imported, unchanged}, grants {imported, unchanged}, default}},
        skipped {<name> [<id>]}, identifiers {<name> {project|global {imported, unchanged}}}, policy
        {project|global {imported, unchanged}}, files_kept}. Exit 4 under the
        read-only switch, 5 when the store cannot be reached.

  migrate [--dry-run|--apply] [--answers FILE]
        Move this agent's file-held configuration into the store, on its
        master node, as one deliberate run: its connections, grants and
        default pointer, identifiers and gate (as rows naming the agent), the
        settings of every capability's project home as settings rows - with
        capabilities/tasks/conveyor.toml folded in as the tasks settings'
        `escalation` table and capabilities/slack/policy.json merged into
        the slack settings' allow-list (an entry both hold keeps the
        service's; each entry only one holds, and each mode the two
        disagree on, asked) - and through each capability's `<name> migrate files` what it keeps
        of its own: automations [[automations]] and engine.timezone as
        definitions and [agents.workers] as harness profiles
        automations-<name>, askproject's profiles folder, whatsapp's and tasks'
        profile files and tasks' worker files as store versions, telegram's
        old worker settings as profiles, agentrooms' agents map as workers,
        and deployment's runtime.json service_policy and placement as policy
        rows naming each node. The default is a dry run, which writes
        nothing: what each part would write, every row the store already
        holds differently, the steps that would bring the contract's tables
        and each capability's own tables to theirs (tables_to_upgrade, by
        owner; a dry run changes nothing in the store, its schema included,
        and reads it as those steps would leave it, a table not made yet as
        empty), each settings value naming a host path, each file
        that differs from its committed copy, each other root of the agent
        on this machine (verify-only, differences named), one node per
        deployment target, and every question code cannot answer - a node
        for a legacy automation, a connection whose stored spec differs
        ("store": the agent adopts the stored spec; "files": the stored spec
        is replaced; {"rename": "<name>"}: the agent's own is written under
        that name and its grants, default and settings `connection` follow
        it, the stored one left to the others), a node to register, an id project.json and .contextkit/config.toml
        disagree on, a slack user or channel only one of policy.json and the
        service settings holds (keep or drop), a slack mode the two disagree
        on. Each question names the path in the --answers JSON its
        answer goes to. --apply refuses (exit 6, nothing written) without a
        database (its own code), without AGENT_NODE_NAME (node_unnamed), on a
        node other than the answers' or the open migration's (node_mismatch)
        or other than the agent's master (not_master), before `migrate
        --global` (global_not_migrated), with a payload older than this
        contract (payload_outdated) or with a question open (question_open).
        A second checkout of the agent - a linked git worktree of its main
        checkout, a root other than the one its open migration started in,
        or a root whose files are behind another root of the same id that
        already keeps the records in the store - only verifies: it names its
        main checkout and differences (second_checkout), writes, removes
        and freezes nothing, and until the agent has moved refuses
        (second_checkout) naming the main checkout. A root in files mode
        whose agent the store already keeps records of, with no checkout of
        it found, refuses the same way and asks `checkout`: answered with
        this root's path, it moves the agent from here.
        It registers the agent and this node as its master on the first
        run, sets migrating_since (from then only this migration writes the
        agent's records and settings, on any node), archives every file git
        does not hold as committed at 0600 under the state home, stops the
        agent's launchd agents here and pauses its services that pause,
        registers the target nodes answered, writes, then reads everything
        back through the store and compares it both ways with what the files
        resolve (exit 7 verify_failed, the agent left frozen). Finalize
        refuses while a target's node is unregistered
        (target_node_unregistered), else sets migrated_at, clears
        migrating_since, writes "records": "store", removes the files (a git
        change to commit) and resumes what it stopped. A journal under the
        state home makes a run interrupted anywhere resume on the next
        --apply; a run on an agent already moved changes nothing. An agent
        whose records were already in the store keeps them: its stale files
        are named, never fail the run, and are removed. One whose project.json
        says "records": "store" while the store in force holds none of its
        grants, identifiers or gate, and its files hold some, is asked
        `records`: "files" writes the files as for an agent in files mode,
        "store" keeps the store and removes them. JSON {ok, project,
        agent_id, node, mode, applied, state, blockers, questions, plan,
        would_write | registered, movers, records_written,
        settings_written, verify, migrated_at, removed, archive, services}.
  migrate --global [--dry-run|--apply] [--answers FILE]
        Write the config home's gate, identifiers, connections, grants (a
        grant's allow_write only, never enabled), default pointer and global
        settings as the global rows every agent inherits, converging the
        store's global rows on them, and set global_migrated_at; the dry run
        lists each row it would add, change or remove and every agent whose
        effective gate would flip (gate_flips), counting only agents that
        read the same database. --apply first brings the contract's tables
        to this code's version and names the steps it ran (tables_upgraded);
        the dry run changes nothing, the schema included, names the steps an
        apply would run (tables_to_upgrade, by owner) and plans as if they
        had run.
        Once set, it changes nothing.
  migrate --discard [--root PATH]...
        On a node that is not the global master: delete the config home's
        record and settings files without reading them. The roots it weighs
        are the ones --report finds plus every root holding an envelope or a
        ContextKit binding below the folders those sit in and below each
        --root. Refused without AGENT_NODE_NAME (node_unnamed), on the master
        node of any of those agents (master_node), before global_migrated_at
        (global_not_migrated), or while one of those roots has not moved or
        has no id (agents_unmigrated, each root named with where it was
        found).
  migrate --report [--root PATH]...
        What remains on this machine and why: each agent root found in the
        machine services' join lists, AgentKit's agent list, the roots
        given, the project here and their git worktrees, read through each
        one's body root - its state, its record and settings files left, an
        open migration's journal, and the lines of its own text that read
        those files directly or name a retired import verb (consumers) apart
        from historical notes that only quote them - assets/, memory/ and
        snapshots/ at the root or body root, and change logs - which are
        counted (historical_notes) and left as they are - and the config
        home's files.

VERIFICATION VERBS
  doctor
        Reconcile machine registry ↔ snapshots ↔ project gate ↔ gitignore
        guard ↔ deny rules ↔ standalone host wiring, including what keeps
        Codex from loading the capability context ↔ project identity; report
        drift, and the project's project_identity state (see path
        --identity). A handed-down id that differs from project.json's is
        drift, as is a store-mode project whose store cannot be used. In store mode `information` lists, never
        as drift, each agent_id holding grants in the store that ContextKit's
        registry (contextkit_agents) does not know (grant_agent_unknown), or
        that the registry is not in the store (agents_registry_absent).
        Exit 0 clean, 7 drift found.
  audit <name> [--from <path>]
        Contract conformance: call the contract verbs, validate output
        shapes, exercise the gate (enabled / disabled / absent) in a
        sandbox, and byte-check the vendored preamble against
        contract/preamble.py. Exit 0 pass, 7 failures (incl. drift) found.
  sync-contract [--check]
        Stamp the canonical shared helpers from contract/preamble.py into the
        fenced regions (`capability core`, `store`, `connections`) of every
        capabilities/*/bin/*. The manager never participates. Idempotent.
        --check reports drift and exits 7 without writing.
  selfcheck
        Prove the repo's own wholeness (the inward companion to audit): the
        enforcer parses, both audit branches (core-only / connection-bearing)
        have a living green example and fire on a deliberate break, every standing
        rule names an enforcement the manager has, and every doc link resolves.
        Fleet capability audit remains a direct dev/source check.
        Exit 0 whole, 7 otherwise.

AUTHORING
  new <name> --source ID [--core-only]
        Create a canonical capability skeleton in that source; generated
        contract regions are stamped by the manager.
  guide [<topic>]
        Without a topic, list manager guides with their titles, previews, and
        exact read commands. With a topic, print that guide's Markdown body.
        Start here for capability authoring, repositories, contract work,
        development, review, sanitizing, and publishing, and for moving
        agents from files to the database (migrate-to-database).

I/O CONTRACT
  Success: JSON on stdout, except the documented Markdown renderers
  (`context --fragment`, `ids`). Failure: {"error": {"code", "message",
  "hint"?}} on stderr. Exit codes: 0 success, 2 auth, 3 not found,
  4 policy (a capability disabled by the gate, or a connection that does not
  allow writes — ask the user, never lift either yourself), 5 network,
  6 input/validation, 7 drift or conformance failures (doctor/audit).
"""

