SERVICE_SUPERVISION_CAPABILITY = "deployment"
# A deliberate exception to "pointers flow down, an asset never names its
# consumer". Someone who installs the manager and one service capability has
# no way to discover that keeping that service running has a standard answer
# at all, and the question arrives the moment the daemon does. So the answer is
# named beside every declared service.
#
# It stays an exception rather than a habit because of how it is written: one
# manager-owned string rendered next to each service, never a sentence copied
# into a capability, and a route rather than a restatement - what supervision
# is and how it works stays entirely with the capability named here.
SERVICE_SUPERVISION_POINTER = (
    "  Keeping it running - on this machine or on a server - is the "
    f"`{SERVICE_SUPERVISION_CAPABILITY}` capability's domain: "
)
CORE_CAPABILITIES = ("askproject", "automations", "deployment", "memory", "tasks")
# The catalogue's core tier, and its only declaration. Which capabilities are
# foundational is a fact about the catalogue, which no single capability is
# part of deciding, so the manager declares it rather than any manifest. Every
# catalogue capability not named here is standard. The tier is a mark readers
# group by, and it acts once: a core capability's first install from the
# official catalogue arrives allowed on the machine and enabled at global
# scope, where that scope holds no entry for it yet. After arrival it is gated,
# enabled and granted exactly as a standard capability is.
# Source verification reads this assignment from the tree it verifies and
# refuses a name that tree's catalogue does not carry.
CAPABILITIES_CONTEXT_INTRO = (
    "Capabilities are self-contained CLIs installed on PATH and "
    "managed by `capabilities`. Use `capabilities list` to inspect the installed "
    "set and project gate, and `capabilities help` for the manager contract. "
    "For an enabled capability, start with `<name> help`; use `<name> doctor` to "
    "prove readiness, `<name> connections` to inspect credential resolution, and "
    "`<name> guide` for its how-to guides. Policy is controlled by "
    "`capabilities enable|disable <name> --project|--global`. When asked to "
    "change policy, first ask whether it applies only to this project or "
    "globally to every project; do not infer scope. Exit 4 is policy — a "
    "disabled capability or a read-only "
    "connection; ask the user, never lift a gate yourself. Global enable "
    "covers ordinary CLI use; a bundled service's init/start/run still requires "
    "explicit project enable."
)

